{"reply":"In **Chapter 8**, Douglass had been sent away after Captain Anthony’s death so that he could be valued and divided as part of the estate. He writes that he “fell to the portion of Mrs. Lucretia” and was therefore “sent immediately back to Baltimore” to live again with Master Hugh.

The return changed his understanding of slavery in two connected ways. First, the valuation had made his status newly vivid: he had been ranked alongside livestock and treated as property. Second, after his return, Lucretia and Andrew died, and the estate—including the enslaved people—passed to strangers. Douglass saw his grandmother, despite her lifelong service, left enslaved and eventually abandoned in a hut. He says this experience deepened his conviction of slavery’s brutality and filled him with “unutterable loathing of slaveholders.”

